<section class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">SIZING & ACCEPTANCE</span><h1>Bandwidth is<br><em>only the beginning.</em></h1><p class="lede">Describe the combined workload before choosing hardware or comparing quotes. Subscribers, packets, flows, churn, logging and failure reserve belong in one capacity discussion.</p></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">THE WORKLOAD PROFILE</span><h2>Six dimensions to bring.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>Traffic shape</h3><p>Busy-hour throughput, packet-size distribution, protocol mix and bursts. A large-packet result does not predict small-packet performance.</p></article><article><h3>Subscriber lifecycle</h3><p>Connected subscribers, new sessions per second, reconnect storms, authentication latency and the distribution of speed plans.</p></article><article><h3>NAT state & churn</h3><p>Concurrent flows, new-flow rate, ports per subscriber, protocol timeouts, pool allocation and failover churn.</p></article><article><h3>Enabled services</h3><p>Shaping, ACLs, routes, encapsulation, accounting and mapping logs. Measure the combination you intend to run.</p></article><article><h3>Operational load</h3><p>Diagnostics, exports, telemetry, retention and backups. Measure management behaviour and dataplane outcomes separately.</p></article><article><h3>Recovery reserve</h3><p>Surviving-node headroom, convergence, reconnection and restoration. A single-node benchmark is not an N+1 design.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="split section"><div><span class="eyebrow">READ THE DEMO CORRECTLY</span><h2>12,864 subscribers.<br>One fictional workspace.</h2><p>The demo provides a large searchable dataset and synthetic graphs to explore the interface. These are not measured engine capacity, paid customer counts or throughput guarantees.</p><a class="button" href="https://demo.liquidnat.com/">Explore the workflow demo ↗</a></div><div class="stack"><article><h3>Evaluate now</h3><p>Search, profiles, reviewed changes, addressing, diagnostic logic, audit history and simulated jobs.</p></article><article><h3>Measure in a live test</h3><p>Loss, latency, shaping, authentication scale, NAT behaviour, convergence and continuity under failure.</p></article><article><h3>Record the conditions</h3><p>Hardware, NIC/firmware, revision, topology, workload, enabled features, thresholds and raw results.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">REPEATABLE EVIDENCE</span><h2>A result you can reproduce.</h2><div class="cards"><article><h3>Healthy-state run</h3><p>Exercise the full service mix with a defined warm-up and measurement interval. Capture resource use and loss as load increases.</p></article><article><h3>Failure-state run</h3><p>Remove dependencies your design claims to tolerate. Measure interruption and recovery separately, then check accounting and ownership after restoration.</p></article></div></section>
